# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# RDR-ART: an entry that has a picture — the caption, the drawing after
# it, and the screen underneath given back whole (#328).
#
# **What this leg is for.** Several of a journal's entries are drawings,
# and until this the reader showed their one-line caption and nineteen
# empty rows. A picture is now the page *after* the caption, so `NEXT`
# walks onto it; it is drawn by plane surgery into the box the program's
# own frame drawer has just put up, and — this is the part that can only
# be wrong on a screen — **in the arrival after the one that queued that
# frame**. A handler's own pixels land the instant it runs and a call
# into the program lands when the batch does, so a picture drawn beside
# the frame goes *under* it. What a leg can hold is the consequence: the
# picture page settles and holds still, and the screen the journal was
# opened over comes back byte for byte.
#
# **A single leg, and it has to be one**, for `rdr-bar.leg`'s reason:
# `N` and `E` reach the program in a seam-off run, so a pair would be
# diffing the program's own answer to two letters as well as the reader.
#
# **A store of its own, and not a picture added to `reader-store.txt`.**
# Four legs in this directory fill the log with `--cite-all-journal` and
# open whatever row the cursor lands on, so giving an existing entry a
# second page would move frames they have pinned - and three of those
# four cannot be run without the code-wheel document, which is exactly
# the kind of change nobody would find out about here.
#
# **The picture is this repository's own and is not a drawing of
# anything** (`reader-art-store.txt`, PLAN.md §6). It is 152x120 — half the
# box, so the centring shows — and it is the four things a reader of one
# can get wrong, side by side:
#
#   * rows 0..39, four bars of thirty-eight pixels, one per stored tone,
#     darkest first. On the screen they are the ramp: white, light grey,
#     dark grey, and the ground itself (`art_ramp` in
#     `seam_journal.cpp`);
#   * rows 40..79, a **one-pixel comb** of ink and paper in alternate
#     columns — which is what the panel's 2x1 average exists for, since a
#     sampler halving it would throw every other line away and leave
#     either a white field or a black one;
#   * rows 80..119, a solid block of ink on paper, x 56..95;
#   * a one-pixel ink border round the whole of it, so the picture's own
#     extent is on the screen rather than inferred from where the ink
#     stops.
#
# **Driven, on the shorter boot** (`--code-wheel-answered`, #291), by
# hand, because the runner does not yet carry that flag for a re-record —
# #293 owes every leg in this directory its re-drive. Same host, same
# disk copy, same `--wall none`, same dump cadence. Measured off those
# stills, at 25 frames:
#
#   * the prompt at 9,625, the digit at 10,025, the entry's text page —
#     full screen, because it was opened from the party's own bar — from
#     10,575, the **picture** from 11,675, and the adventuring screen
#     again from 13,200;
#   * a picture page settles in about 75 frames where a twenty-row text
#     page takes about 230 (#305), and it is the same mechanism in both:
#     one batch for the clear, the frame and the bar, and then one
#     arrival more. For a page of text that second part is five passes of
#     four rows; for a picture it is a single blit.
#   * the four tone bars, looked at once at 2x on the still at 12,400:
#     white, light grey, dark grey, black, left to right, with the comb
#     under them reading as a grey field of vertical lines and the block
#     under that solid. Which is the ramp doing what it says, and the
#     only part of this a person had to judge.
#
# **And the picture that matters was looked at too**, off the
# maintainer's own ingested edition rather than off this store, because
# no committed file may carry a page of anybody's book: Entry 4's cloth
# map and the atlas's three maps, on the whole screen and halved in the
# roster panel. `docs/journal.md` §11.6 has what that run said and what
# it did not settle.
about a picture of an entry: the caption, the drawing after it, and the screen given back
kind single
program START.EXE
store tests/visual/reader-art-store.txt
both code-wheel
# The boot #291 left, as `rdr-bar.leg` takes it: the seam on and the
# challenge already answered, so there is no wheel screen in this script.
code-wheel-answered
seam journal
until 300000000
every 25

# The prompt rather than the log, because a picture is a fact about an
# entry and not about what the game has cited: F1, the number, Return,
# `NEXT` onto the drawing, and `EXIT` out through the program's own
# composer.
key L@7550
key A@7800
key F1@9600
key 4@10000
key Return@10400
key N@11600
key E@13000

# Each screen, holding still with nothing pressed.
same 9700-9975
same 10100-10375
same 10600-11575
same 11700-12975

# And the screen the journal was opened over, back again after it, with
# nothing excepted — the same `equal` `rdr-bar.leg` earned when the
# `Notes` splice began handing the bar highlight back (#330). Nothing
# here touches that bar, which is why this leg may borrow the claim: a
# page opened from the prompt goes out through the program's own screen
# composer, and 13,200 onwards is the 9,000-9,600 still exactly.
same 13250-15075
equal 9575 13500
